<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riksdag of the Estates</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as four estates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of the Estates was the form of the Swedish parliament in which representatives sat in four separate estates: the nobility, the clergy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and the peasantry. Its inclusion of a peasant estate was unusual among European assemblies of the time. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> describe the four estates as part of the governing framework through which the crown mobilised resources for its wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riksdag-of-the-estates.jpg" alt="Riksdag of the Estates" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_coat_of_arms_of_Sweden_(without_mantle).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The four estates followed the historic lines of division in Swedish society, and until its dissolution the assembly was the highest authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> after the king. Its peasant estate, Bondeståndet, did not include the whole peasantry. Crofters, lodgers, seasonal labourers and other unlanded or semi-landed groups had no seat, and of the three kinds of bönder only two were represented: the skattebönder, tax-paying yeomen who owned their land, and the kronobönder, who farmed Crown land. The frälsebönder, who farmed land owned by the nobility, were held to be represented by their landlords.</p><p>The meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arboga">Arboga</a> in 1435 is usually counted as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a>, though there is no sign that the farmers attended it. The first true meeting was probably the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> in 1436, after the death of the rebel leader Engelbrekt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The assemblies settled many turning points. In 1517 the regent Sten Sture the Younger and the Privy Council deposed Archbishop Gustav Trolle at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a>; at Västerås in 1527 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> as the state religion, and in 1544 an order of royal succession ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elective monarchy</a>. The name Riksdag was first used at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arboga">Arboga</a> in 1561. At Söderköping in 1595 Duke Charles was chosen regent in place of King Sigismund, a Catholic who was also king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and in 1612, after lobbying by the Lord High Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna">Axel Oxenstierna</a>, the nobility gained the right to hold all higher offices.</p><p>The first open conflict among the estates came in 1650, when the three lower estates attacked the nobility's privileges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-queen-of-sweden">Queen Christina</a> used the quarrel to secure her cousin Charles Gustav as her heir. In 1680 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> enacted a large reduction, the return to the Crown of lands granted to the nobility, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, confirmed in 1693. The constitution of 1719 restored the Riksdag's powers. The Union and Security Act of 1789 abolished most noble privileges, and in 1809 the Riksdag elected Charles XIII king after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iv-adolf">Gustav IV Adolf</a> had been deposed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1866 all the estates voted to dissolve themselves and form a new assembly, Sveriges Riksdag. The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobility">Nobility</a> remained a quasi-official body until 2003. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1809, the estates met at Porvoo that year to confirm their new allegiance, and the Diet of Finland kept the Swedish forms. It was not assembled again until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> summoned it in 1863, and in 1905 it passed an act forming a new unicameral parliament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Riksdag of the Estates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
